# Give a bare-handed monk Two-Weapon Tempest and read the Brawl row twice.
#
# THE POINT. Two-Weapon Tempest is the largest two-weapon payoff in the game:
# +10 to speed, which the character sheet renders as +50 percentage points
# (src/Values.cpp, src/Sheet.cpp:193-200). Character::HasFeat grants a monk
# Two-Weapon Style for the express purpose of letting him buy it, and until
# inc-nie the feat then did nothing with empty hands, because the whole
# two-weapon block was gated on two Item pointers and fists are not Items.
#
# WHY THIS BUILD. Tempest wants Two-Weapon Style plus melee base attack bonus 9
# (src/FeatTab.cpp:1022-1026). A monk attacks at 75% in melee, so eleven monk
# levels reach 8 and no monk can ever qualify alone. Character::GetBAB sums the
# classes and a warrior attacks at 100%, so Monk 1 / Warrior 10 reaches 10 --
# eleven levels, which is the game's cap. Monk 1 is what carries
# CA_UNARMED_STRIKE, and so the fists, the Two-Weapon Style grant and
# TwoFistFighting().
#
# THE CHARACTER COMES FROM A FILE, NOT FROM A SEED. This script used to build
# him with a copy of tools/keys/monk-brawl-speed.keys and then empty his hands.
# It cannot: a seed-pinned character is not reproducible across module changes,
# and the weapon chargen hands him is exactly the field that moves. Commit
# bef32c3 replaced his long sword with a quarterstaff, the stow that used to
# empty his hands was refused for size, and the Brawl row this script exists to
# read stopped being drawn at all (bd inc-sls0). He now comes from
# tools/fixtures/chars/lizardfolk-monk-barehand-seed1.sav, which holds the same
# character with his hands already empty; tools/fixtures/README.md says why a
# character who must stay himself has to be loaded rather than rolled. Levels
# still come from wizard mode's Free Advancement switch, as in
# tools/keys/prestige-blackguard5.keys.
#
# RUN IT WITH THE FIXTURE NAMED, or there is no character at all:
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-18.dat \
#   INCURSION_LOAD=tools/fixtures/chars/lizardfolk-monk-barehand-seed1.sav \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/monk-tempest.keys 1
#
# THE THREE DUMPS ARE THE WHOLE OUTPUT, in this order, because
# tools/check_two_fist_feats_live.sh reads them by number: 0001-sidebar-monk1,
# 0002-sheet-before, 0003-sheet-after. Do not add a fourth above them.

# The session starts in play mode with the saved character already standing on
# the saved map, so there is no chargen and nothing to settle.
#
# THE SEARCH IS NOT FILLER. tools/headless.sh reports NO GAMEPLAY (exit 5)
# unless a turn actually completes, and this script otherwise spends every key
# inside menus. The 's' buys the one turn that makes this a session. Same
# reason, same key, as tools/keys/load-char-sheet.keys.
s

# The sidebar is the regression oracle for inc-dzz: two Punch lines, equal
# to-hit numbers either side of the slash, and full Strength on both.
@dump:sidebar-monk1

w       # wizard mode
y       # "Enter wizard mode?" -- yes
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
DOWN DOWN
SPACE   # Free Advancement: NO -> YES
ESC

d       # character sheet
M       # [M]ulticlass
@choose "Warrior"

# Ten warrior levels. A warrior is offered a bonus feat at every level, and
# general feats and attribute bumps arrive on the character-level schedule, so
# all of them are guarded by name rather than by letter.
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Warrior"
@while "choose a Warrior Feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Warrior"
@while "choose a Warrior Feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Warrior"
@while "choose a Warrior Feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Warrior"
@while "choose a Warrior Feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Warrior"
@while "choose a Warrior Feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Warrior"
@while "choose a Warrior Feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Warrior"
@while "choose a Warrior Feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Warrior"
@while "choose a Warrior Feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Warrior"
@while "choose a Warrior Feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d

# Warrior 9 now: melee base attack bonus 9, which is Tempest's gate. The Brawl
# row here is the BEFORE measurement.
@expect "Warrior 9"
@dump:sheet-before

# One more level, and this time the warrior's bonus feat is spent on Tempest.
# The level-up menu truncates a feat name to 18 characters, so the text asked
# for here is one character shorter than the feat's real name.
@until "class do you wish to advance" G
@choose "Warrior"
@expect "choose a Warrior Feat"
@choose "Two-Weapon Tempes"
@while "choose a Warrior Feat" a
@while "Focus on which Weapon" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" c
@while "You feel hardier" ESC
@until "Skill Ratings" d
@expect "Warrior 10"
@dump:sheet-after
@quit
